    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d159">

                <pb facs="0417" n="351" sameAs="frus:frus1961-63v18/pg351"/>

                <head>159. Memorandum From Robert W.
                        Komer of the National Security Council Staff to President
                        Kennedy<anchor n="0"
                        xml:id="d159-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1963-02-20">February 20,
                        1963</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>Here is an interim response to your 18 February query on “what are we planning to
                    do re Iraq?”<anchor n="1" xml:id="d159-f2"/></p>
                <p>We are going to (1) name an Ambassador shortly, since the new regime reversed
                    Kassem’s policy of expelling ambassadors from those countries recognizing
                    Kuwait; (2) offer technical help if needed; (3) encourage sending more Iraqi
                    students to US military schools (we’ve had about 100 here since 1958
                    revolution).</p>
                <p>But we think it might create the wrong impression on the new regime if we
                    embraced them more fulsomely just now; it’s better to let <pb facs="0418"
                        n="352" xml:id="pg352"/> them come to us. If Iraq needs money, we think IPC
                    will make an advance; over the longer run it has plenty of oil revenue.</p>
                <p>Most experts here (and Embassy Moscow) doubt the USSR, despite its obvious unhappiness on the crackdown on Iraqi
                    Communists, will cut its aid, and thus thrust Iraq back toward us. Rather the
                    Soviets will try to salvage as much of their position as they can. We’d also
                    have trouble with MAP for a neutralist Iraq,
                    because of the precedent it would set for other Arab countries and the annoyance
                    to Israel. But we’ll look at military sales if occasion offers.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#bold">R. W.
                            Komer</hi><anchor n="2" xml:id="d159-f3"/></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d159-f1" n="0" type="source" xml:id="d159-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Kennedy Library, National Security
                    Files, Countries Series, Iraq, 1963. Secret.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d159-f2" n="1" xml:id="d159-f2-content">
                <p>On February 18, President Kennedy had sent
                    Secretary of State Rusk a memorandum that
                    reads: “What are we planning to do re Iraq? If the Russians cut off their aid,
                    are we planning to make any offers to them?” (Ibid.)</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d159-f3" n="2" xml:id="d159-f3-content">
                <p>Printed from a copy that bears this typed signature.</p>
            </note>
